    {
      "text": "We are told \\( y^3 \\) varies inversely with \\( \\sqrt[3]{z} \\).\n\nThat means:\n\n\\[\ny^3 \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{z} = k\n\\]\nfor some constant \\( k \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: Find \\( k \\).**\n\nWhen \\( y = 2 \\) and \\( z = 1 \\):\n\n\\[\ny^3 = 8, \\quad \\sqrt[3]{z} = 1\n\\]\nSo:\n\\[\n8 \\cdot 1 = k \\implies k = 8\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Use \\( k \\) to find \\( z \\) when \\( y = 4 \\).**\n\nWhen \\( y = 4 \\):\n\n\\[\ny^3 = 64\n\\]\nEquation:\n\\[\n64 \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{z} = 8\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sqrt[3]{z} = \\frac{8}{64} = \\frac{1}{8}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Solve for \\( z \\).**\n\nCube both sides:\n\n\\[\nz = \\left( \\frac{1}{8} \\right)^3 = \\frac{1}{512}\n\\]\n\n---\n\nThe answer is \\(\\frac{1}{512}\\).\n\nThe answer is \\(\\frac{1}{512}\\)",
